<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-20835] linkea_init() does not validate leh_len against the buffer actually read</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20835</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;linkea_init() (lustre/obdclass/linkea.c) checks the LINK_EA_MAGIC and leh_reccount/leh_overflow_time, but never checks the on-disk leh_len header field against ld_buf-&amp;gt;lb_len, the actual byte count __mdd_links_read() (lustre/mdd/mdd_dir.c) read for the xattr. linkea_next_entry() (lustre/include/lustre_linkea.h) then uses leh_len alone to bound iteration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If leh_len on disk were ever larger than the real buffer (corruption, not reachable from any known race in normal operation), every ordinary consumer of a file's linkEA &amp;#8211; rename, unlink, fid2path, not only LFSCK &amp;#8211; would iterate past the allocated buffer rather than stopping, before LFSCK gets a chance to repair the corruption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Suggested fix: in linkea_init(), reject leh_len &amp;gt; ld_buf-&amp;gt;lb_len (and less than sizeof(struct link_ea_header)) alongside the existing magic/reccount checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not a live bug in any current code path found &amp;#8211; both write-side helpers (linkea_add_buf, linkea_overflow_shrink) maintain leh_len consistent with the buffer by construction, and trusted.link is not writable by an unprivileged client. Filed as a defensive hardening gap: the read path should not trust the length field for the LFSCK-repair case it exists to guard against.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
